@Tutorial(time: 75) {
    @Intro(title: "Cross-Format Integration") {
        Connect XMI, JSON and native Swift data through one shared metamodel.

        In this tutorial, you'll move project data between an EMF-based desktop tool (XMI), a web editor (JSON) and a Swift application, detect and reconcile the changes that each tool makes, and generate the REST, client and event code that ties the tools together.

        All files of this tutorial live in a directory named `integration`. Each terminal step starts in its parent directory and changes into `integration` first.

        @Image(source: "cross-format-integration.svg", alt: "Cross-format integration pipeline: XMI and JSON are converted into each other, ATL maps to a web view and refines models, and MTL generates Swift code and APIs")
    }

    @Section(title: "Understanding Format Integration Challenges") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Systems often hold the same data in several formats. XMI comes from EMF-based tools, JSON from web applications, and native apps want typed Swift values. Each format spells classes, references and enumerations differently.

            The key to integration is to make one metamodel the authoritative definition of the data. Every file in every format is then an instance of that metamodel, and the modelling tools can validate, convert and compare them.

            @Image(source: "format-ecosystem.svg", alt: "The Ecore metamodel at the centre: XMI and JSON files are validated and converted against it, and Swift code is generated from it")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Describe the integration scenario.

                @Code(name: "integration/integration-overview.md", file: workflow-03-step-01-scenario.txt)

                A desktop planning tool saves XMI, a web editor works with JSON, and a Swift app needs native types. The table lists every data flow and the tool that performs it.
            }

            @Step {
                Create the shared metamodel.

                @Code(name: "integration/ProjectManagement.ecore", file: workflow-03-step-02-shared-metamodel.ecore)

                The `id` attributes are marked as identifiers (`iD="true"`), so tools can match objects across files. Dates are `EDate` attributes, which XMI and JSON both write as ISO 8601 text.
            }

            @Step {
                Validate and inspect the metamodel.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-03-step-02-check-metamodel.sh)

                Every later step relies on this metamodel, so check it first. The inspection lists its classes and enumerations, and the statistics count its attributes, references, containments and opposite pairs.
            }

            @Step {
                Record how each format represents the data.

                @Code(name: "integration/format-constraints.md", file: workflow-03-step-03-constraints.txt)

                XMI and JSON both name classes and use the same fragment paths for references, so a conversion keeps everything. Swift needs generated types. A transformation is only needed when the structure must change.
            }

            @Step {
                Plan the integration architecture.

                @Code(name: "integration/integration-architecture.md", file: workflow-03-step-04-architecture.txt)

                The plan assigns each concern to a tool: conversion and validation to `swift-ecore`, queries to `swift-aql`, structural mappings and consistency rules to `swift-atl`, and code generation to `swift-mtl`.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "XMI to JSON Bridge") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The desktop tool saves XMI, but the web editor needs JSON. Changing the format of a model is a conversion, not a transformation: `swift-ecore convert` reads the model with its metamodel and writes the same objects in the other format.

            Web dashboards often want a different shape as well, such as names instead of references and computed totals. That is a mapping between two metamodels, which is the job of an ATL transformation.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Save the XMI data of the desktop tool.

                @Code(name: "integration/project-data.xmi", file: workflow-03-step-05-xmi-data.xmi)

                The model holds an organisation with departments, team members and two projects. References such as `#//@members.0` are EMF fragment paths: the first object of the `members` reference of the root object.
            }

            @Step {
                Convert the XMI file to JSON and validate the result.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-03-step-06-convert-to-json.sh)

                With `-m`, the conversion keeps the classes, enumeration literals and references of the metamodel. The validation confirms that the JSON file is an instance of the same metamodel, and `swift-ecore compare` finds no difference between the two files. Each JSON object names its class in `eClass`.
            }

            @Step {
                Query the JSON data.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-03-step-06-query-json.sh)

                AQL navigates the JSON file as it navigates the XMI file, following references and their opposites, so both formats answer the same queries in the same way.
            }

            @Step {
                Define a compact view for web clients.

                @Code(name: "integration/ProjectAPI.ecore", file: workflow-03-step-07-api-metamodel.ecore)

                Apart from containment, the `ProjectAPI` metamodel has no references: owners and assignees are names, dependencies are task identifiers, and each project carries its task totals.
            }

            @Step {
                Write the transformation to the web view.

                @Code(name: "integration/ProjectToAPI.atl", file: workflow-03-step-07-project-to-api.atl)

                The rules map an organisation to a catalogue, each project to a summary and each task to a task summary. The `isOpen` helper and the collection operations compute the totals.
            }

            @Step {
                Run the transformation.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-03-step-08-run-project-to-api.sh)

                The transformation reads the JSON file and writes the web view as JSON. Validating the result against `ProjectAPI.ecore` confirms that it is a correct instance of the view.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "JSON to Swift Integration") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The web editor changes the data and saves it as JSON. Before other tools use the file, it is validated against the metamodel, so invalid edits stop at the boundary.

            A Swift application can then read the same JSON with types that are generated from the metamodel, so the compiler checks every access and the types stay in step with the model.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Save the JSON data that the web editor changed.

                @Code(name: "integration/updated-project-data.json", file: workflow-03-step-09-updated-json.json)

                In the web editor, the team completed TASK-003, started TASK-004, ticked off milestone MS-002, activated PROJ-002 and added TASK-008 with a new assignment.
            }

            @Step {
                Validate the update and reject an invalid one.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-03-step-10-validate-updated-json.sh)

                The valid update passes. A copy that uses the status `STARTED`, which `TaskStatus` does not define, fails with an error for each offending task, so no other tool receives it.
            }

            @Step {
                Write the template that generates Swift types.

                @Code(name: "integration/GenerateSwiftModels.mtl", file: workflow-03-step-11-swift-generation.mtl)

                The model of this template is the metamodel itself. It generates a `Codable` struct per class, a Swift enum per enumeration with the literal names as raw values, and a `ModelReference` type for the `$ref` objects of the JSON format.
            }

            @Step {
                Write a Swift program that uses the generated types.

                @Code(name: "integration/ProjectReport.swift", file: workflow-03-step-12-project-report.swift)

                The program decodes a JSON model file with `JSONDecoder` and prints the progress of each project. It resolves the assignee references by their fragment paths.
            }

            @Step {
                Generate the types, compile the program and run it.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-03-step-12-json-to-swift-pipeline.sh)

                The same program reads the web editor's file and the file converted from the desktop tool's XMI, because both are instances of the metamodel the types were generated from.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Bidirectional Synchronisation") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Changes must also flow back: the desktop tool needs the web editor's edits as XMI. Synchronisation needs three things: knowing what changed, keeping the rules of the domain intact, and converting without loss.

            Comparing models rather than files makes the format irrelevant, a refining transformation applies the rules of the domain, and a round trip followed by a comparison proves that the conversion keeps every value.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Plan change detection and reconciliation.

                @Code(name: "integration/change-detection.md", file: workflow-03-step-13-change-detection.txt)

                Objects are matched by class and identifier, and a domain rule decides what happens when edits contradict each other: a milestone is complete exactly when all of its deliverables are.
            }

            @Step {
                Compare the original model with the update.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-03-step-14-compare-versions.sh)

                The comparison reads the XMI and the JSON file with the metamodel and reports each change of the web editor: `~` for a changed attribute or reference, `+` for an added object. With `--summary`, it counts the objects of each class in both versions.
            }

            @Step {
                Write the reconciliation rule.

                @Code(name: "integration/ReconcileProject.atl", file: workflow-03-step-15-reconcile-project.atl)

                In refining mode, ATL copies the whole model and applies only the rules that match. `ReconcileMilestone` matches the milestones whose `completed` flag contradicts their deliverables and corrects the flag.
            }

            @Step {
                Reconcile, convert back to XMI and check the round trip.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-03-step-16-test-synchronisation.sh)

                The comparison shows that the rule changed nothing but the flag of MS-002. The conversion validates the XMI file for the desktop tool before writing it, and the round trip through JSON reproduces the original model without any difference.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "API Integration Layer") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Live integration needs interfaces as well as files: a REST API for web clients, a Swift client for native apps, and change events for real-time collaboration.

            Generating all three from the metamodel keeps them consistent with each other and with the files of the earlier sections.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Write the REST API template.

                @Code(name: "integration/GenerateRestAPI.mtl", file: workflow-03-step-17-rest-api-generation.mtl)

                Every class with an identifier becomes a resource with list, get, create, update and delete operations. The template writes an OpenAPI description, data transfer objects that use Foundation only, and route stubs for a Vapor server. Square brackets of the generated text are written as `['['/]` and `[']'/]`, because a bare `[` starts an MTL tag.
            }

            @Step {
                Write the Swift client template.

                @Code(name: "integration/GenerateSwiftAPI.mtl", file: workflow-03-step-18-swift-api-generation.mtl)

                The generated client is an actor with an `async` method for each operation of each resource. It uses the generated data transfer objects and `URLSession`.
            }

            @Step {
                Write the event API template.

                @Code(name: "integration/GenerateEventAPI.mtl", file: workflow-03-step-19-event-api-generation.mtl)

                The generated `ModelEvent` enumeration has a case per resource class. Events encode to JSON for any transport, and the `ModelEventBus` actor delivers them to all subscribers as an `AsyncStream`.
            }

            @Step {
                Generate the APIs and check them.

                @Code(name: "Terminal", file: workflow-03-step-20-generate-apis.sh)

                The data transfer objects, the client and the events type-check together. The route stubs need the Vapor package of a server project. Checksums show that regenerating produces identical files.
            }
        }
    }

    @Assessments {
        @MultipleChoice {
            Which tool should turn an XMI model into JSON for a web editor that uses the same metamodel?

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                `swift-ecore convert` with the metamodel

                @Justification(reaction: "Exactly right!") {
                    Only the format changes, not the structure. A conversion with `-m` keeps the classes, enumeration literals and references of the metamodel.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                An ATL transformation that copies every class

                @Justification(reaction: "That does more work than needed!") {
                    ATL is for mappings between metamodels, such as the `ProjectAPI` view. A copy transformation would only repeat what a conversion does.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                A text editor and careful search and replace

                @Justification(reaction: "Too fragile!") {
                    Hand edits easily break references and enumeration literals. The conversion reads and writes the model through its metamodel.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            Why does the Swift program read both the converted file and the web editor's file?

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Because JSON files are always simpler than XMI files

                @Justification(reaction: "Simplicity is not the reason!") {
                    Both JSON files have the same structure, whatever their size.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                Both files are instances of the metamodel from which the Swift types were generated

                @Justification(reaction: "Perfect!") {
                    The metamodel defines the data in every format, so types generated from it decode every valid JSON model file.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Because `JSONDecoder` ignores keys that it does not know

                @Justification(reaction: "That would hide errors!") {
                    Unknown keys are not what makes this work. The generated types match the metamodel, and validation keeps the files in line with it.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            What does `swift-ecore compare` match objects by?

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Their line numbers in the files

                @Justification(reaction: "Lines depend on the format!") {
                    The two files may be in different formats, so line numbers say nothing about the model.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                Their class and identifier, regardless of the file format

                @Justification(reaction: "Correct!") {
                    The comparison reads both files with the metamodel and matches objects by class and identifier, so it reports changes per object and feature.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Their position in the containment hierarchy only

                @Justification(reaction: "Positions change!") {
                    A new or removed object shifts the positions of its siblings. Identifiers stay the same.
                }
            }
        }

        @MultipleChoice {
            How should contradictory edits from different tools be handled?

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Always keep the most recent change

                @Justification(reaction: "Too simplistic!") {
                    The most recent edit may break a rule of the domain, such as a milestone marked complete while a deliverable is still in progress.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: true) {
                Apply documented domain rules, for example with a refining transformation

                @Justification(reaction: "Excellent approach!") {
                    A rule such as "a milestone is complete exactly when its deliverables are" can be applied automatically, and a comparison shows exactly what it changed.
                }
            }

            @Choice(isCorrect: false) {
                Always resolve them by hand

                @Justification(reaction: "Automation is often possible!") {
                    Many contradictions follow from rules that a transformation can apply. Manual resolution is for genuine disagreements.
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
